Tallow-Jangle
Ingredients: SURF ROCK + BRITPOP + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Electric Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Born when a blood-rivalry wedding between aerialists and lion tamers shattered into an all-night rooftop guitar duel above the big top.

Tallow-Jangle sounds like a rain-drenched North Sea carnival at three in the morning: soaking-wet vintage spring reverb and blistering Dick Dale surf stabs collide with the sneering, cigarette-burned vocal swagger of 1990s Britpop, smothered under the shivering atmospheric dread of circus ghosts, frayed highwires, and damp greasepaint.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–164 BPM. The rhythm marries the relentless, galloping double-kick surf rumble with a heavy, syncopated Northern pub stomp, suddenly dropping into woozy, waltz-timed free-falls.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Dorian modes cross-pollinated with surf-rock flat-fifths and theatrical minor-major ninth chords, evoking seaside Victorian music halls decayed by brine.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Submerged Spring Cavitation: Amps run into twin vintage outboard reverb tanks driven until they produce metallic water-splash pops on every transient.
  2. Trapeze Doppler Suspension (Impossible Technique): Guitar cabs are sealed inside zinc cisterns beneath circus rings while ribbon microphones suspended from 40-foot aerial silks are swung in centrifugal parabolas overhead, warping the guitar’s pitch purely through the physical momentum of swinging bodies.
  3. Tallow-Damped Pick Attack: Electric guitar strings are slathered near the bridge in theatrical mutton-tallow wax, resulting in a stinging, choked percussive twang before the note explodes into feedback.
- Dynamics: Songs open with airy, wind-whistled swagger—a solitary guitar strumming mod-chords on a roof—before erupting into deafening, panic-speed surf-tremolo runs that abruptly cut to dead air, haunted only by shivering amp hum.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre ignited in October 1996 atop the slate roofs of the Winter Gardens in Blackpool during the ill-fated nuptials of Jack Delamere (heir to a dynasty of third-generation French-Irish trapeze artists) and Clara Vane (daughter of a fierce English menagerie family). When an antique silver punchbowl was smashed across the ringmaster's jaw during the vows, both factions dragged their tube amplifiers and battered offset guitars up the maintenance ladders onto the rooftops. As rain turned to sleet, they hurled sneering insults through vocal megaphones and traded furious surf licks across chimney stacks. The youth subculture that followed—dubbed "The Riggers"—wears tailored three-button Britpop suits shredded at the shoulders, moth-eaten velvet circus capes, smeared white theatrical powder, and climbing boots caked in wet sawdust. Their gatherings occur on precarious architectural heights above coastal towns, armed with portable battery-powered valve heads and handheld telescopes.

Central Instruments: Fender Jazzmaster and Jaguar electric guitars (strung with flatwound heavy gauges and drenched in spring reverb), Burns London 12-string guitars, overdriven Vox AC30s, upright circus harmoniums, battered Ringo-style trap kits, and megaphone vocals.

For Fans Of: The Last Shadow Puppets, Man or Astro-man?, The Coral, The Horrors

